/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · life-money: practical money admin in Australia */
WB.L("lodging-your-tax-return", "life", "beginner", "Doing your own tax return, step by step",
  "Every year millions of Australians lodge a tax return from their couch in under an hour. Here is how to do it calmly, claim what you are entitled to, and keep the records that protect you if the ATO comes asking.",
  [
    ["Know the calendar", "A self-lodged tax return is generally due by 31 October, but wait until your income statement is marked 'tax ready'.",
      "The Australian financial year runs from 1 July to 30 June. If you lodge your own return, it is generally due by 31 October. A registered tax agent can often give you extra time, provided you are on their books before that date.\n\nDon't rush in on 1 July. Your employer, bank and health fund are still sending their information to the ATO. Wait until your income statement in myGov is marked 'tax ready', usually late July or August, and most of your details will be waiting for you. This lesson is general information, not tax advice, so check your situation at ato.gov.au."],
    ["Set up and check prefill", "myTax pre-fills much of your return, but you should check every figure against your own records.",
      "The simplest way to lodge is myTax, the ATO's free online return. Create a myGov account, then link the ATO as a service. You prove who you are with questions about a past notice of assessment, your bank account or a payment summary, or with a digital ID. Turn on two-step sign-in and never share your login.\n\nSalary, tax withheld, bank interest and health insurance details then fill in automatically. Treat that as a starting point, not a guarantee. Compare it with your payslips and add anything missing: a second job, side hustle, dividends or foreign income. The ATO matches data from many sources, so omissions are often caught later, with interest and penalties."],
    ["Three rules for deductions", "You can claim a work expense only if you paid for it, it directly relates to earning your income, and you have a record.",
      "Deductions reduce your taxable income, so it is tempting to be generous. The ATO's rules boil down to three tests. First, you spent the money yourself and were not reimbursed. Second, the expense relates directly to earning your income, not private life. Third, you have a record to prove it.\n\nIf something is partly for work and partly personal, like a phone or laptop, you can claim only the work share. Ordinary commuting, everyday clothes and childcare are generally not deductible. The ATO publishes occupation guides, from nurses to tradies, spelling out what people in each job can usually claim."],
    ["Keep records as you go", "Keep digital records as you go, and hold them for five years from the day you lodge.",
      "The best time to organise tax records is the moment you buy something, not the following October. Photograph each work receipt into one folder, or use the free myDeductions tool in the ATO app. It lets you snap receipts and log car trips and expenses through the year, then upload them to myTax.\n\nGenerally you must keep records for five years from the date you lodge. For working from home, the ATO expects evidence of the hours you actually worked, such as a timesheet, roster or diary, not a rough guess made up at year's end. The methods and rates are reviewed from time to time, so check the current rules."],
    ["After you lodge", "Read your notice of assessment line by line, and fix any mistake by amending the return.",
      "When you submit, myTax shows an estimate of your refund or the amount you owe. The ATO then issues a notice of assessment in your myGov inbox. Read it rather than just checking the bottom line. It shows your taxable income, the tax on it, any Medicare levy, study loan repayments and credits for tax withheld. If you owe money and can't pay by the due date, contact the ATO early, as they can often arrange a payment plan.\n\nIf you find an error, you can amend through myTax, usually within two years of your notice of assessment. For complex affairs, a registered agent can be worth the fee, and you can check one on the Tax Practitioners Board register at tpb.gov.au. You, not the agent, are responsible for what your return says."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "If you lodge your own tax return, when is it generally due?", ["30 June","31 August","31 October","31 December"], 2, "A self-lodged return is generally due by 31 October, though waiting for 'tax ready' data makes lodging easier."],
    [1, "Why should you check prefilled figures in myTax before lodging?", ["Prefill can miss income or contain errors","The ATO charges for each change","Prefill is always accurate, so checking is optional","Only payslips need to be checked, not bank records"], 0, "Prefill is a starting point, and left-out income is often caught later with interest and penalties."],
    [2, "Which is NOT one of the three tests for claiming a work deduction?", ["You spent the money and were not reimbursed","The expense directly relates to earning your income","You have a record to prove it","The item cost more than a set minimum amount"], 3, "The tests are about who paid, the link to earning income, and having a record, not a minimum cost."],
    [3, "Generally, how long should you keep your tax records?", ["One year from purchase","Until you receive your refund","Ten years from purchase","Five years from when you lodge"], 3, "Records are generally kept for five years from the date you lodge, and storing them digitally as you go makes that easy."],
    [4, "Where can you check that a tax agent is registered?", ["The Fair Work Ombudsman","The Tax Practitioners Board","Services Australia","The agent's own website"], 1, "The Tax Practitioners Board register is the official check, and you remain responsible for your return either way."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Most of your tax return fills itself in. The risk sits in what it leaves out."});

WB.L("super-housekeeping", "life", "advanced", "Super housekeeping: find, combine and check your fund",
  "Many Australians have super scattered across funds they barely remember, each quietly charging fees. A once-a-year check-up can tidy it up, but there are traps to look out for before you hit 'combine'.",
  [
    ["The scattered super problem", "Old super accounts keep charging fees, so it pays to find them even though stapling now slows new duplicates.",
      "Picture someone who worked at a café, then a retail chain, then an office. Before the rules changed, each employer could open a new default super account. Each one may charge administration and investment fees, and sometimes insurance premiums, nibbling at small balances.\n\nSince late 2021, stapling means your existing fund generally follows you to a new job unless you choose otherwise. But old accounts don't vanish by themselves. The quickest way to see them all is myGov, with the ATO linked: it lists every account in your name, plus lost super and unclaimed super held by the ATO. This is general information, not financial advice."],
    ["Check insurance first", "Rolling an account into another fund normally ends its insurance, so compare cover before you combine.",
      "This is the trap that catches people. Many super accounts include default insurance: cover if you die or become totally and permanently disabled, and sometimes income protection. When you roll an account into another fund, the old cover normally ends. If your health has changed since you joined, you may struggle to get equivalent cover elsewhere.\n\nBefore consolidating, download the insurance details for each account and compare the type of cover, the amounts and the cost. Cover in an account that has stopped receiving contributions can also be switched off after a period of inactivity unless you ask to keep it. Decide deliberately, rather than letting a rollover decide for you."],
    ["Keep, or combine", "Some accounts are worth keeping, and once you choose, consolidating through myGov is free and simple.",
      "Defined benefit funds, common in older public sector and some corporate schemes, calculate your benefit by formula rather than balance, so leaving can mean giving up valuable entitlements. Some employers also pay into a specific fund with cheaper fees or tailored insurance. Funds can no longer charge exit fees, but investments may be sold and re-bought during a transfer, and your money can be out of the market for a few days.\n\nOnce you have chosen a fund, select the accounts and the receiving fund in myGov, and the request is sent electronically. Transfers take days to weeks. Check that old accounts show zero, tell your employer which fund to use, and avoid anyone charging to 'find your lost super'. The official tools are free."],
    ["Is your fund any good?", "Compare funds on long-term net returns after fees, using the ATO's YourSuper tool.",
      "A single account is tidy, but it should also be a good one. The ATO's YourSuper comparison tool ranks MySuper products, the default options most people are in, by fees and net returns over several years.\n\nUnder the annual performance test, products that underperform a benchmark must write to their members to say so, and repeat failures can be closed to new members. If you get one of those letters, take it seriously.\n\nCompare long-term returns after fees, not one good year, and check that the investment mix suits how long you have until retirement. Fees that look small as a percentage can add up to a large sum over decades, thanks to the same compounding that grows your balance."],
    ["Beneficiaries and check-ups", "Super does not automatically follow your will, so nominate beneficiaries and review your super yearly.",
      "Super is generally held in trust, so it does not automatically follow your will. Most funds let you nominate who receives your balance and any insurance payout if you die. A valid binding nomination generally directs the trustee to pay the people you name, while a non-binding one is only a guide.\n\nSome binding nominations lapse after a set period unless renewed, and marriage, separation or a new child can make an old nomination wrong. Set a yearly reminder to check contributions, fees, insurance, investment option and beneficiaries. Moneysmart.gov.au has plain-language guides to every step."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What does 'stapling' mean in Australian super?", ["Your existing fund generally follows you to a new job","The government merges all your accounts","You must change funds with every job","Your employer picks a new fund each year"], 0, "Stapling keeps your existing fund with you, which slows the creation of duplicate accounts."],
    [1, "What is a key risk of rolling an old super account into another fund?", ["Your tax file number is cancelled","Its insurance normally ends","You lose access to myGov","Your employer stops paying super"], 1, "Cover in the old account normally ends on rollover, so compare insurance before you combine."],
    [2, "Why might someone keep a defined benefit fund rather than roll it over?", ["It always charges the lowest possible fees","It cannot be seen or found in myGov at all","The benefit uses a formula that can be valuable","It pays the highest interest rate on savings"], 2, "A formula-based benefit can be worth more than the account balance, so leaving may mean giving it up."],
    [3, "What must happen when a MySuper product fails the annual performance test?", ["The ATO refunds all fees","The fund must notify its members","Members are automatically moved","Nothing, it is just kept private"], 1, "Underperforming products must notify members, and repeat failures can be closed to new members."],
    [4, "Why is a beneficiary nomination important?", ["It lowers your fees","It increases your insurance","It is needed to make contributions","Super may not follow your will"], 3, "Super is held in trust, so a valid nomination guides who receives it if you die."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"You may own super accounts you've forgotten, and they may be quietly costing you."});

WB.L("cutting-household-bills", "life", "beginner", "Switching providers: beating the loyalty tax",
  "Staying with the same provider for years can quietly cost you. A little comparison, a phone call and a calendar reminder can trim your household bills without changing how you live.",
  [
    ["The loyalty tax", "Long-term customers often pay more than new ones, so check your deals once a year.",
      "Picture two neighbours with identical houses and habits. One signed up with an energy retailer a decade ago and never looked again. The other compares plans each year. The first often pays noticeably more, because a discount expired or cheaper plans arrived that they never moved to. This pattern, sometimes called the loyalty tax, shows up across energy, phone, internet and insurance. Providers rely on inertia.\n\nThe fix is not constant switching. Choose one month, perhaps around tax time, and review every regular bill in a single sitting. Start with your latest bills: usage, daily supply charge, rates, any time-of-use pricing and your solar feed-in tariff."],
    ["Compare energy officially", "Use the free government comparison sites, and look past headline discounts.",
      "For electricity and gas, use free government comparison sites rather than commercial ones that may be paid by retailers. In New South Wales, Queensland, South Australia, Tasmania and the ACT, the Australian Energy Regulator runs Energy Made Easy at energymadeeasy.gov.au. In Victoria, use Victorian Energy Compare. Western Australia and the Northern Territory have different market arrangements, with fewer choices in many areas.\n\nEnter your usage or upload a bill to see plans at your address. In many states retailers must show how offers compare with a government reference price. Look past headline discounts: conditional discounts that vanish if you pay late can make a plan dearer than it looks."],
    ["Switching and right-sizing", "Switching is usually painless, and you should match phone and internet plans to what you actually use.",
      "Changing energy retailers usually involves no physical work. The same poles, wires and meter supply you, and only the company billing you changes. The new retailer arranges the transfer. Check for exit fees and contract end dates. You can usually keep your mobile number, and month-to-month plans leave you free to go. Sometimes just telling your provider you are thinking of leaving gets you a better plan.\n\nMany people pay for more mobile data or home internet speed than they use. Check your usage in your provider's app, then choose a plan with a modest buffer. On the NBN, typical evening speeds matter more than maximums. Cancel streaming services and subscriptions you no longer use."],
    ["Hardship help", "If paying bills is hard, ask early for the hardship team, and check what concessions you can get.",
      "If paying bills is becoming hard, act before disconnection notices arrive. Energy retailers must have hardship programs, which can include payment plans, advice on cutting usage and protection from disconnection while you take part. Telcos have financial hardship policies too. Ask for the hardship team by name.\n\nState and territory governments offer energy concessions and rebates to eligible people, such as pensioners, healthcare card holders and some low-income households. These are not always applied automatically, so check your state government's website. Services Australia can tell you which cards and payments you may qualify for. The free National Debt Helpline, on 1800 007 007, connects you with a financial counsellor who can negotiate with providers for you."],
    ["When things go wrong", "Complain to the provider first, then take unresolved problems to the free ombudsman.",
      "Billing errors, surprise fees and botched transfers do happen. Contact the provider, explain the problem clearly and ask for a complaint reference number. Keep notes of every call, including the date and the name of the person you spoke with.\n\nIf the problem isn't fixed, you can go to an independent ombudsman for free. For phone and internet, that is the Telecommunications Industry Ombudsman. For energy and water, most states have their own energy and water ombudsman scheme, so check which applies in your state. Ombudsmen can investigate and, in many cases, require providers to fix the problem or pay compensation. This is general information; check moneysmart.gov.au and your state's consumer affairs website."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What does the 'loyalty tax' refer to?", ["A government tax on long-term customers","A fee charged for switching providers","Loyal customers often paying more than new ones","A discount for staying with one provider"], 2, "Providers rely on inertia, so a yearly check keeps you off outdated, pricier plans."],
    [1, "Which site does the Australian Energy Regulator run for comparing energy plans in several states?", ["Victorian Energy Compare","Energy Made Easy","myGov","YourSuper"], 1, "Energy Made Easy is a free government comparison site, unlike commercial sites that may be paid by retailers."],
    [2, "What usually happens physically when you switch electricity retailer?", ["A new meter must be installed","Your power is cut off for a day","Nothing, only the billing company changes","An electrician must visit to reconnect you"], 2, "The poles, wires and meter stay the same, so switching is usually painless."],
    [3, "If you are struggling to pay an energy bill, what should you ask for?", ["The retailer's hardship team","A new meter","A higher discount on late payments","To be disconnected"], 0, "Hardship programs can include payment plans and protection from disconnection, and asking early gives you more options."],
    [4, "Where can you take an unresolved phone or internet complaint for free?", ["The Australian Financial Complaints Authority","The Australian Taxation Office","The Fair Work Ombudsman","The Telecommunications Industry Ombudsman"], 3, "Complain to the provider first, then escalate to the free, independent ombudsman."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Two identical homes, two very different bills. The gap has a name."});

WB.L("making-an-insurance-claim", "life", "intermediate", "Making an insurance claim that sticks",
  "You pay premiums for years hoping you never need them. When something finally goes wrong, how you handle the first days of a claim can make a real difference to the outcome.",
  [
    ["Prepare before disaster", "Know your policy, sum insured and excess before you need to claim, because underinsurance is common.",
      "The best time to prepare for a claim is a quiet weekday, long before a storm or break-in. Download your product disclosure statement, the long document that sets out what your policy covers, and read the sections on exclusions and how claims work.\n\nCheck your sum insured for home and contents. Underinsurance is common, because people guess the value of their belongings and fall well short. Many insurers offer online calculators for rebuilding or replacement costs. Film each room and store the video in the cloud, along with receipts for expensive items. Note your excess, the amount you pay towards each claim. This is general information, not advice; read your own policy."],
    ["Make safe, then document", "Prevent further damage if it is safe, and photograph everything before you clean up.",
      "Imagine coming home to water pouring through the ceiling. Safety comes first: turn off water or power if it is safe, and call emergency services if anyone is in danger. Policies generally expect reasonable steps to prevent further damage, such as a tarp or an emergency plumber, and those costs are often covered.\n\nBefore you throw anything away, photograph and film the damage, including serial numbers and brand labels. Keep damaged items until the insurer says you may dispose of them. After a car crash, exchange names, contact details, registrations and insurer details, photograph the scene, collect witness details and don't admit fault, as that is for insurers to work out. Report theft to police and record the event number."],
    ["Lodge and keep a log", "Notify your insurer promptly, keep a written log, and be completely honest.",
      "Contact your insurer as soon as practical, through their app, website or claims line. Delays can complicate a claim, especially if damage worsens. You will receive a claim number; write it on every document and email.\n\nStart a simple claim log on paper or in a notes app. Record the date and time of every call, who you spoke to and what was agreed. Follow up important calls with a short email confirming what was said.\n\nBe honest and accurate in everything you tell the insurer. Exaggerating a claim, even slightly, can give the insurer grounds to refuse it or cancel your policy, and it can have serious legal consequences."],
    ["Assessors and settlement", "You can get your own quotes, and a cash settlement usually ends the insurer's responsibility for repairs.",
      "For larger claims, the insurer may send an assessor or ask for quotes from builders. You can generally supply your own quotes, and it is reasonable to ask for a copy of the assessor's report.\n\nInsurers may offer to manage repairs through their own builders, or offer a cash settlement. Cash can seem attractive, but check that it would cover the real cost of fixing the damage with a licensed tradesperson. Once you accept it, the insurer usually has no further responsibility for the repairs. Ask how the figure was calculated. Keep receipts for temporary accommodation and emergency costs, as some policies cover these up to a limit."],
    ["Decisions and disputes", "Ask for decisions in writing with reasons, then complain internally before going to AFCA.",
      "Insurers that subscribe to the General Insurance Code of Practice commit to keeping you updated, deciding within set timeframes and explaining their reasons. If your claim is denied, ask in writing for the decision, the policy wording relied on and any reports used. Sometimes a refusal rests on a misunderstanding that extra evidence, like a receipt or tradesperson's report, can fix.\n\nIf you still disagree, make a formal written complaint, use the word 'complaint', and say what outcome you want. Most complaints should get a response within about 30 calendar days. If you are unhappy or hear nothing, lodge a complaint with the Australian Financial Complaints Authority at afca.org.au. It is free for consumers and independent of insurers, and its decisions can bind the insurer."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why is it worth checking your sum insured before anything goes wrong?", ["It lowers your excess automatically","Underinsurance is common and costly","It cancels policy exclusions","It is needed to lodge a tax return"], 1, "People often guess their belongings' value and fall short, so checking in advance avoids a nasty surprise."],
    [1, "What should you do before throwing away damaged items?", ["Nothing, throw them out quickly","Sell them online to recoup some money","Repair them yourself before photographing","Photograph them and wait for the insurer's OK"], 3, "Evidence and damaged items let the insurer assess the claim, so document first and discard later."],
    [2, "Why is exaggerating a claim, even slightly, a bad idea?", ["It can lead to a refused claim or cancelled policy","It only delays the payment by some weeks","It lowers your excess on later claims","It is overlooked if you keep a claim log"], 0, "Honesty protects both your claim and your policy, and a log keeps your account accurate."],
    [3, "What is a risk of accepting a cash settlement?", ["You must pay tax on all of it at once","Your excess doubles on future claims","The insurer usually owes no further repairs","All your other policies are cancelled"], 2, "Check the amount covers the real repair cost first, because accepting usually ends the insurer's obligation."],
    [4, "Where can you take an insurance dispute after the insurer's internal process?", ["The Fair Work Ombudsman","The Tax Practitioners Board","The Australian Taxation Office","The Financial Complaints Authority"], 3, "AFCA, the Australian Financial Complaints Authority, is free for consumers and independent of insurers, and it follows a written internal complaint."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"What you do in the first hours after damage can decide how a claim ends."});

WB.L("comparing-personal-loans", "life", "advanced", "Comparing loans: the real cost of borrowing",
  "The interest rate in the ad is only part of the story. Fees, terms, balloon payments and add-ons can turn a cheap-looking loan into an expensive one. Here is how to read the numbers like a lender.",
  [
    ["Total cost beats the rate", "Compare loans by the total amount repayable, because the headline rate ignores fees.",
      "Two car loans are advertised side by side. One has a lower rate; the other has no upfront fee. Which is cheaper? You can't tell from the rates alone. True cost is interest plus every fee: establishment, monthly account, early repayment and late fees. The most useful single number is the total you will repay over the life of the loan. Ask lenders for it directly.\n\nLenders advertising rates on many consumer loans must show a comparison rate, which rolls most fees into one percentage. But it is based on a standard example loan. If yours is smaller or shorter, fixed fees weigh more and your real cost can be higher. It also excludes some costs, such as late fees. Treat it as a guide. This is general information, not credit advice."],
    ["Secured, fixed or variable", "Secured loans usually cost less but put an asset at risk, and fixed rates trade flexibility for certainty.",
      "A secured loan uses an asset, often the car you are buying, as security, so the lender can repossess it if you default. Because the lender's risk is lower, secured loans generally have lower rates than unsecured ones.\n\nA fixed rate stays the same for the term, which makes budgeting easy, but fixed loans often charge break costs or limit extra repayments. A variable rate can rise or fall, but variable loans frequently allow extra repayments without penalty, which can save significant interest if you plan to pay off early.\n\nAsk each lender three questions: can I make extra repayments, can I pay out the loan early, and what will each cost? The answers can matter as much as the rate."],
    ["Balloons and long terms", "Smaller repayments from longer terms or balloon payments usually mean more interest overall.",
      "Lenders and car dealers advertise the weekly repayment, because it feels affordable. Two common ways to shrink it are a longer term and a balloon payment. A balloon, also called a residual, is a large lump sum due at the end of the loan.\n\nBecause part of the debt is pushed to the end, you pay interest on a bigger balance for longer, so the total cost rises. When the balloon falls due, you need the cash, or must refinance, or sell the car, which may by then be worth less than you owe. Longer terms work the same way: smaller repayments, more total interest. Always compare loans on total cost, not the weekly repayment."],
    ["Dealer finance and add-ons", "Get your own quote before accepting dealer finance, and question any add-on insurance.",
      "Car yards and big retailers often offer finance on the spot. It is convenient, but easy to focus on the car and skip comparing. Get pre-approval or a quote from your own bank or credit union before you shop. It gives you a benchmark and strengthens your hand.\n\nWatch for add-ons bundled into the loan, such as gap insurance, extended warranties or loan protection insurance. Sold this way, they have historically been poor value for many buyers, and when financed you pay interest on them too. Recent rules restrict how some add-on insurance can be sold, including a deferred sales period, but you can still simply say no. Ask for the price of the car and the loan separately."],
    ["Lenders, credit and method", "Lenders must check a loan is not unsuitable, and you should compare offers side by side before deciding.",
      "Under responsible lending laws, licensed lenders must make reasonable inquiries about your income, expenses and needs, and must not offer an unsuitable loan, such as one you could repay only with substantial hardship. Be truthful. Each formal application is usually recorded on your credit report, so ask about eligibility checks first. Avoid payday lenders where possible. People on low incomes may qualify for the No Interest Loan Scheme.\n\nFor each offer, write down the amount, term, rate, comparison rate, every fee, extra-repayment rules and any balloon, plus the total repayable. Ask whether you could cope if income fell or rates rose. Then sleep on it: 'today only' pressure is a warning sign."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "When might your real cost be higher than the comparison rate suggests?", ["When you make every single repayment on time","When your loan is smaller or shorter than the example","When the loan carries no fees of any kind at all","When the interest rate is variable, not fixed"], 1, "The comparison rate uses a standard example loan, so a smaller or shorter loan can cost more than it shows."],
    [1, "Why do secured loans generally have lower rates than unsecured ones?", ["An asset backs it, lowering the lender's risk","They never carry any establishment fees","They are always fixed rate loans for the term","Government schemes subsidise the interest"], 0, "Security lowers the lender's risk, but it means you could lose the asset if you default."],
    [2, "What is a balloon payment?", ["A monthly account fee charged by the lender","A refund paid out at the end of the loan","A large lump sum due at the end of the loan","A penalty charged for paying the loan early"], 2, "Balloons shrink repayments but raise total interest, and the lump sum still has to be found later."],
    [3, "What is a smart step before accepting car yard finance?", ["Accept the add-ons to secure the deal","Sign the same day to lock in the deal","Judge it by the weekly repayment alone","Get your own quote or pre-approval first"], 3, "A benchmark from your own bank lets you judge whether the dealer's offer is actually good."],
    [4, "Under responsible lending laws, what must licensed lenders do?", ["Approve every application that meets a minimum","Make reasonable inquiries about your situation","Offer the lowest rate available in the market","Avoid looking at your credit report"], 1, "Lenders must check your income, expenses and needs, which is why truthful applications protect you."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A cheap-looking loan can cost more than a dearer one. The advertised rate hides why."});

WB.L("getting-out-of-debt", "life", "intermediate", "Getting out of debt: a practical plan",
  "Debt can feel like a fog where every bill blurs into one. The way out starts with turning that fog into a list, then using free help that many people never know exists.",
  [
    ["Make a list, set priorities", "Write down every debt, then protect housing, utilities and other high-consequence debts first.",
      "People in debt often avoid opening envelopes, and that is human. But you can't plan around numbers you haven't seen. Set aside an hour and log in to every account. For each debt, write who you owe, the balance, the interest rate, the minimum repayment and the due date. Include credit cards, loans, buy now pay later, overdue bills, money owed to family, and any ATO or Centrelink debt.\n\nThen sort them. Priority debts carry the most serious consequences: rent or mortgage, energy and water, and a car loan if you need the car for work. Fines and tax debts can escalate too. Cover these first. Never take out a new high-cost loan to keep up with another debt. This is general information, not financial advice."],
    ["Ask for hardship help", "Contact lenders early and ask for a hardship variation, and get any agreement in writing.",
      "If you can't meet repayments, contact the lender before you fall far behind. Under the National Credit Code, you can ask for a hardship variation if illness, job loss or other changed circumstances make repayments hard. Options can include a pause, reduced repayments for a period or a longer loan term. Lenders must consider the request and respond.\n\nEnergy, water and phone companies have hardship programs too. Ask for the hardship team, explain what happened and what you can realistically afford, and get any agreement in writing. Hardship arrangements can be noted on your credit report, but that is generally far less damaging than missed payments and defaults."],
    ["Avalanche or snowball", "Pay minimums on everything, then put all spare money on one debt, using whichever method you will stick with.",
      "With essentials covered and minimums being paid, direct any extra money at one debt at a time. There are two popular methods.\n\nThe avalanche method targets the highest interest rate first, which usually saves the most money. The snowball method targets the smallest balance first, whatever the rate, giving a quick win that builds momentum.\n\nWhen one debt is cleared, roll its repayment into the next, so the amount you throw at debt keeps growing. Mathematically the avalanche usually wins, but the snowball can suit people who need motivation. The best method is the one you will actually stick with."],
    ["Consolidation traps", "Combining debts helps only if the new loan is cheaper and you stop adding new debt.",
      "A consolidation loan rolls several debts into one, ideally at a lower rate. A balance transfer moves credit card debt to a card with a low or zero rate for a promotional period. Both can work, but there are traps.\n\nPromotional rates end, and the balance often reverts to a high rate. New purchases on a balance transfer card may attract full interest. A longer loan can lower repayments but raise the total cost. And if you keep using the old cards, you can end up with the new loan plus fresh debts.\n\nIf you consolidate, consider cancelling or lowering the limits on old cards, and plan to clear the balance before any promotional period ends."],
    ["Free help and last resorts", "Free financial counsellors can help; debt agreements and bankruptcy are serious last resorts.",
      "The free, independent National Debt Helpline, on 1800 007 007 or at ndh.org.au, connects you with counsellors who can explain options and negotiate with creditors. Some private debt management and credit repair businesses charge large fees for things you can often do free, like requesting hardship. Speak to a free counsellor before signing anything. You can also complain to the Australian Financial Complaints Authority, also free.\n\nWhen debts are truly unmanageable, federal law offers debt agreements and bankruptcy, administered by the Australian Financial Security Authority (afsa.gov.au). They can stop collection action but are recorded on public registers and your credit report, and can limit borrowing. Get independent advice first. Afterwards, build a small emergency fund so the next repair doesn't go on a card."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Which is usually considered a priority debt?", ["A store card balance","A credit card with a low limit","Money owed to a streaming service","Rent or mortgage repayments"], 3, "Falling behind on housing or utilities has the most serious consequences, so they come first."],
    [1, "Under the National Credit Code, what can you ask a lender for if you are struggling?", ["A higher credit limit","A hardship variation","A cash refund of interest","A new credit card"], 1, "Asking early for a hardship variation gives you options such as a pause or reduced repayments."],
    [2, "What does the avalanche method target first?", ["The debt with the highest interest rate","The debt with the smallest balance","The debt you took out most recently","The debt owed to a family member"], 0, "Clearing the highest-rate debt first usually saves the most interest overall."],
    [3, "What is a common trap with balance transfers?", ["The new card always charges more than the old one","The promotional rate ends and a high rate applies","Old cards are cancelled automatically by the bank","They always shorten the time to repay the debt"], 1, "Promotional rates end, so plan to clear the balance before then and stop adding new debt."],
    [4, "What is the National Debt Helpline?", ["A paid credit repair service","A government-owned lender","A free counselling service","A private debt collection agency"], 2, "Free, independent counsellors can explain your options and negotiate with creditors, so try them before any paid service."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Debt feels like fog. One hour and one page can start to clear it."});

WB.L("big-purchases-bnpl", "life", "beginner", "Buying big things without the debt trap",
  "A new fridge, a laptop, a couch: big purchases often come with a tempting offer to pay later. Here is how buy now pay later, interest-free deals and rent-to-own really work, and a better way to buy.",
  [
    ["How pay-later works", "Pay-later offers make big prices feel small, and the risks come from stacking plans and missing payments.",
      "At checkout, a friendly line appears: four easy payments, no interest. A thousand-dollar purchase suddenly looks like a few hundred. That shift in feeling is the point. Splitting a cost makes it easier to say yes, and research suggests people tend to spend more when paying feels less painful.\n\nBuy now pay later typically means four payments over about six weeks, the first upfront. Many charge no interest, earning money from retailers' fees and customers' late or account fees. Since mid-2025, BNPL has been regulated as credit, so providers need a credit licence and must check the product isn't unsuitable for you. The risks are several plans across providers, missed-payment fees and credit report problems. This is general information, not financial advice."],
    ["Interest-free fine print", "Interest-free deals are only free if you clear the balance in time, because afterwards high interest and fees apply.",
      "Furniture and electronics stores often advertise long interest-free periods, perhaps a year or more, through a store card or finance partner. These can be useful if you pay the full amount within the period.\n\nThe trap is what happens if you don't. Any remaining balance usually starts attracting a high rate, often similar to a credit card. Many of these products also charge a monthly account fee, and some an establishment fee, so the deal isn't truly free. The account may stay open for future purchases.\n\nBefore signing, ask three questions: what is the rate after the interest-free period, what are the fees, and what is the minimum repayment?"],
    ["Rent-to-own leases", "Consumer leases can cost far more than the item's price, so look at the No Interest Loan Scheme first.",
      "Some businesses offer appliances, furniture or electronics on a weekly lease, sometimes promoted to people on Centrelink payments with deductions taken straight from their payments. These leases can cost much more than the retail price, and at the end you may not own the item. ASIC has repeatedly warned about the high cost of some leases.\n\nIf you need an essential like a fridge or washing machine and can't afford it, look at alternatives first. The No Interest Loan Scheme, run by Good Shepherd with community partners, offers loans for essential goods to eligible people on low incomes, with no interest or fees. Services Australia may also offer an advance payment if you receive certain payments."],
    ["Pause and find the total", "Wait before big purchases, and judge the total cost, not the instalment.",
      "A simple habit stops most regretful purchases: wait. For anything over an amount you choose, perhaps a few hundred dollars, give yourself 48 hours, or a week for bigger items. If you still want it, it's probably a considered decision rather than an impulse.\n\nNext, work out the total cost, not the instalment. Add up every payment and fee if you use credit. Then ask a blunt question: if I had to pay the full price today from my own money, would I still buy it? If not, the payment plan is making the decision for you. Check that your existing repayments plus this one still leave room for rent, bills and food."],
    ["Save first, buy smarter", "Saving first through a sinking fund avoids interest and gives you bargaining power.",
      "The cheapest way to buy something big is usually to save first. A sinking fund is money set aside regularly for a known future cost, such as a laptop, car registration or a replacement fridge. Divide the expected price by the pay cycles until you need it, and automate a transfer into a separate account on payday. You earn a little interest instead of paying it, and cash can give you bargaining power. Ask politely whether paying upfront gets a better price.\n\nCompare prices across retailers, and be wary of 'sale' prices that aren't real bargains. Consider quality secondhand goods. New goods come with automatic consumer guarantees under the Australian Consumer Law, which can matter more than an extended warranty."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Since mid-2025, how is buy now pay later regulated in Australia?", ["It remains unregulated under Australian law","As a form of credit requiring a licence","Only by the retailer selling the goods","As a type of general insurance product"], 1, "BNPL providers need a credit licence and must check the product isn't unsuitable for you."],
    [1, "What usually happens to a store finance balance left after the interest-free period?", ["It is quietly written off by the store","It is converted into a store gift card","It is paid off by the finance partner","It attracts a high interest rate"], 3, "The deal is only free if you clear the balance in time, and account fees may apply as well."],
    [2, "What is a cheaper alternative to rent-to-own for essential goods on a low income?", ["A short-term payday loan","A second consumer lease","The No Interest Loan Scheme","A new store credit card"], 2, "The No Interest Loan Scheme offers loans for essentials with no interest or fees to eligible people."],
    [3, "Which question helps test whether a payment plan is driving a decision?", ["How small is the weekly payment on offer?","Would I buy it if I paid the full price today?","Can the plan be stretched over a longer period?","Is this the last one left in stock?"], 1, "If you wouldn't pay the full price from your own money, the instalments are doing the persuading."],
    [4, "What is a sinking fund?", ["A super fund that has stopped paying out","A type of buy now pay later account","A government grant for household appliances","Money set aside regularly for a known cost"], 3, "Saving a little each payday lets you buy outright, avoid interest and sometimes negotiate a better price."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A $1,000 price tag can be made to feel like $250. Here's the design behind it."});

WB.L("building-an-emergency-fund", "life", "beginner", "Building an emergency fund from scratch",
  "A blown tyre, a vet bill, a sudden gap between jobs: life sends invoices without warning. An emergency fund turns those moments from a crisis into an inconvenience, and you can start with very little.",
  [
    ["A buffer changes everything", "An emergency fund stops surprise costs becoming high-interest debt, and about three months of essentials is a common target.",
      "Two people face the same expensive car repair. One pays from savings, feels annoyed and moves on. The other uses a credit card, pays interest for months, and when the next surprise hits, the debt grows. The difference is not income or intelligence; it is a buffer.\n\nA common target is about three months of essential expenses: rent or mortgage, bills, groceries, transport and minimum debt repayments. Casual workers, freelancers and single-income households may want more. Starting from zero, break it down: first enough for one surprise bill, then one month of essentials, then two, then three. Add up essentials from bank statements or use Moneysmart's budget planner. This is general information, not financial advice."],
    ["Where to keep it", "Keep the fund separate, reachable within a day or so, and earning some interest.",
      "An emergency fund needs to be accessible quickly, but not so close that you dip into it for takeaway. A separate high-interest savings account, ideally not linked to a card, is a common choice. Many pay bonus interest only if you meet conditions, such as a monthly deposit and no withdrawals, so read the terms. Losing the bonus in a month you use the fund is fine.\n\nIf you have a home loan with an offset account, money held there reduces your interest. Deposits in authorised banks, building societies and credit unions are protected by the government's Financial Claims Scheme up to a limit; check the current limit at fcs.gov.au."],
    ["Finding the money", "Automate a small transfer on payday, then add windfalls and savings from cut costs.",
      "The most reliable way to save is to make it automatic. Set up a regular transfer to your emergency account on payday, before you can spend the money. Start with an amount that doesn't hurt, even a small sum each pay, and raise it when you can.\n\nAdd windfalls such as a tax refund, a bonus or money from selling things you no longer use. When you cut a cost, like cancelling an unused subscription or switching to a cheaper phone plan, redirect that saving straight into the fund so it doesn't disappear into everyday spending. Some banks offer round-up features that move small change from each purchase into savings, which can help build the habit."],
    ["Rules and refills", "Decide in advance what counts as an emergency, and refill the fund promptly after using it.",
      "An emergency fund only works if it survives until an emergency. Decide in advance what counts: unexpected, necessary and urgent things, like a failed hot water system, an urgent dental bill, a car repair you need for work, or essentials after losing your job. Holidays, gifts and planned bills like car registration or insurance are not emergencies. Predictable costs belong in a separate sinking fund. Frequent raids may mean your budget needs adjusting, not that you lack willpower.\n\nUsing the fund is not failure; it did its job and you avoided debt. Then restart your automatic transfers and set a realistic timeframe to refill it. Review your target yearly, as your essential costs change."],
    ["If you have nothing yet", "If an emergency arrives first, ask for payment plans and use cheaper help than payday loans.",
      "Sometimes the emergency arrives before the fund does. Avoid the most expensive options first, such as payday loans and some consumer leases. Ask the business you owe about a payment plan; many tradespeople, vets, utilities and hospitals offer them.\n\nIf you receive certain Centrelink payments, Services Australia may give you an advance payment, which you repay from future payments, and there are crisis payments for specific severe situations; check eligibility at servicesaustralia.gov.au. The No Interest Loan Scheme can help eligible people with essential costs. The National Debt Helpline, on 1800 007 007, offers free financial counselling. Once the problem is handled, start your fund, even with a tiny first deposit."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is the main job of an emergency fund?", ["To invest in shares for long-term growth","To replace your superannuation savings","To keep surprise costs from becoming debt","To pay for holidays and gifts each year"], 2, "A buffer lets you absorb surprises without borrowing, and about three months of essentials is a common target."],
    [1, "What protects deposits in authorised Australian banks up to a limit?", ["The Financial Claims Scheme","The Australian Consumer Law","The National Credit Code","The Fair Work Act"], 0, "The government's Financial Claims Scheme protects eligible deposits up to a limit, which you can check at fcs.gov.au."],
    [2, "What is the most reliable way to build savings?", ["Saving whatever is left at month's end","Waiting for a windfall","An automatic transfer on payday","Checking your balance daily"], 2, "Automating the transfer moves money before you can spend it, so saving doesn't depend on willpower."],
    [3, "Which of these is best paid from a sinking fund rather than an emergency fund?", ["A burst hot water system","Annual car registration","An urgent dental bill","Essentials after a job loss"], 1, "Predictable, planned costs belong in a separate sinking fund so they don't drain your buffer."],
    [4, "If you receive certain Centrelink payments, what might Services Australia offer in a pinch?", ["A free credit card with a high limit","A guaranteed grant to cover any bill","A bank loan guaranteed by the government","An advance repaid from future payments"], 3, "An advance payment is cheaper than payday loans, though it reduces your later payments."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"The same car repair can cost two people very different amounts. Why?"});

WB.L("income-protection-insurance", "life", "intermediate", "Income protection: insuring your pay",
  "Your ability to earn may be worth more than your house or car, yet many people insure those and not their income. Here is how income protection works, and the questions to ask before you buy or keep it.",
  [
    ["Insuring your pay", "Income protection replaces part of your income, typically a monthly benefit of up to about 70 percent, if illness or injury stops you working.",
      "Ask people for their most valuable asset and they name their home or car. But for a working adult, the pay they expect to earn over coming decades is often worth far more. Illness or injury that stops you working for months can drain savings fast while bills keep arriving.\n\nIncome protection pays a regular monthly benefit while you can't work because of a covered illness or injury. It replaces only part of your income, commonly up to around 70 percent in many current policies, which keeps an incentive to return to work. It differs from life insurance, which pays on death, and total and permanent disability cover, which pays a lump sum. Definitions of being unable to work vary, so read the product disclosure statement. This is general information, not financial advice."],
    ["Waiting and benefit periods", "The waiting period sets when payments start, and the benefit period sets how long they can last.",
      "Two choices shape both the cost and usefulness of a policy. The waiting period is how long you must be unable to work before benefits start, often 30, 60 or 90 days, or longer. A longer wait usually means a cheaper premium, which can suit you if you have sick leave or an emergency fund to bridge the gap.\n\nThe benefit period is how long payments can continue once they start, such as two years, five years or until a set age. Longer periods cost more but protect against long-term illness, which does the most financial damage. Think about how long your savings and leave could realistically carry you, then compare combinations."],
    ["Inside or outside super", "Cover inside super is convenient but often more limited than a standalone policy.",
      "Many people already have income protection through their super fund without realising it. Check your fund's website or annual statement. Cover inside super is paid from your super balance, which spares your weekly budget but reduces your retirement savings. It often has shorter benefit periods and stricter definitions than standalone policies. Default cover can be switched off if your account has been inactive for a period, or for some younger members and low balances, unless you opt in.\n\nA standalone policy, bought directly or through an adviser, can usually be tailored more closely. You can hold both, but benefits may be offset so you can't claim more than the limits allow. Compare before you add or cancel anything."],
    ["Tax and premiums", "Premiums for cover held outside super are generally tax-deductible, and benefits are generally taxable.",
      "The tax treatment surprises some people. If you pay premiums for income protection held outside super, the ATO generally lets you claim them as a deduction, because the policy replaces taxable income. The flip side is that benefit payments are generally taxable income too.\n\nPremiums paid from within super aren't claimed in your personal return. If a policy bundles income protection with other cover, such as life insurance, only the income protection part is usually deductible, and your insurer should show the split.\n\nPremiums can be stepped, rising with age, or level, starting higher but rising more slowly. Both generally increase over time. Check the ATO website for the rules that apply to you."],
    ["Disclosure and other safety nets", "Answer application questions honestly, and know where sick leave, workers compensation and government support end.",
      "When applying, you'll be asked about your health, job, income and lifestyle. Answer fully and honestly. Under Australian law you must take reasonable care not to make a misrepresentation, and inaccurate answers can let the insurer reduce a payout, refuse a claim or cancel the policy. Get quotes from more than one insurer or speak to a licensed adviser, and compare disability definitions, benefit amounts, waiting and benefit periods and exclusions, not just price.\n\nIncome protection is one layer. Paid personal leave covers short absences. Workers compensation may cover work-related injury or illness. Services Australia income support is means-tested and usually far below a working wage. Knowing where these end helps you choose how much private cover makes sense."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How does income protection differ from life insurance?", ["It pays a lump sum to your family if you die","It replaces part of your pay when you can't work","It covers damage to your home, car or contents","It pays only your compulsory super contributions"], 1, "Income protection replaces part of your income while you can't work, whereas life insurance pays on death."],
    [1, "What usually happens to premiums if you choose a longer waiting period?", ["They double","They are not affected","They become tax-free","They are usually cheaper"], 3, "A longer wait means you cover the gap yourself, with sick leave or savings, so the premium is lower."],
    [2, "How are premiums for income protection inside super usually paid?", ["From your weekly pay","By the government","From your super balance","By your employer on top of super"], 2, "Cover inside super spares your weekly budget but reduces your retirement savings."],
    [3, "What is the general tax treatment of income protection held outside super?", ["Premiums generally deductible, benefits generally taxable","Premiums and benefits are both completely tax-free","Premiums are always taxed and benefits always deductible","Neither premiums nor benefits have any tax effect"], 0, "The policy replaces taxable income, so premiums are generally deductible and benefits generally taxable."],
    [4, "Why must you answer application questions honestly?", ["It guarantees you a lower premium","A claim can be reduced or refused","It removes the waiting period","It is only a routine formality"], 1, "Inaccurate answers can let the insurer reduce a payout, refuse a claim or cancel your policy."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Your future pay may be your biggest asset, and most people leave it uninsured."});

